==English==

===Etymology===
From English dialectal {{term|wanky|lang=en}}, alteration of {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|wankel||unstable, shaky|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|wancol||unstable|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*wankulaz||swaying, shaky, unstable}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*wankōną||to sway, be unsteady}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*wa(n)k-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*wek-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*wag-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*weg-||to swing, be unsteady, slant, be crooked}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|wankle||wonky|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|wankel||shaky|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Wankelmut||fickleness, inconstancy, vacillation|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|vanke||to wander|lang=da}}. See also {{l|en|wankle}}.

